    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders account for only 10.4% of volume with 4 sets, and their strength index was flat at 103. While not alarming, shoulders are a key muscle for upper-body balance and aesthetics in a hypertrophy program and could benefit from an extra set or two."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "triceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Triceps received 3 sets and 8.4% of volume with a flat strength index. Given that triceps make up a large portion of upper-arm mass, keeping them stagnant while chest progresses may create a downstream imbalance over time."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Add one to two sets for biceps next period, bringing them to at least 4 to 5 sets total — this is the most underdeveloped group relative to your muscle-building goal and the flat strength index confirms they need more stimulus.",
    "Increase shoulder sets from 4 to at least 5 or 6 next period and aim to push the shoulder strength index above 103 — a small volume bump here can break the current plateau.",
    "Continue the progression pattern on chest and legs, which are both trending upward — maintain or slightly increase load on those movements to sustain the momentum seen this week.",
    "Keep total weekly sets at or above 28 and aim to nudge total tonnage higher as strength improves — with your current bodyweight and training age, this volume is appropriate but there is room to grow progressively over the coming weeks."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strong week with chest, back, and legs all progressing on the strength index; arms and shoulders were flat and under-volumed, making them the priority to address next period."
}
